[00:01.50]The chart above clearly demonstrates the total number of fans 
[00:05.50]followers paying close attention to the Microblog of 
[00:09.50]entertainment stars from 2017 to 2018.
[00:15.73]In 2017,
[00:18.44]the number was at a low level of 12.8 billion,
[00:21.41]while within one year,
[00:23.35]it reached 16.7 billion,
[00:25.48]a rise of 3.9 billion.
[00:28.32]Undoubtedly,
[00:29.54]the diagram demonstrates the social phenomenon of idol worship,
[00:35.14]which is prevalent among teenagers nowadays.
[00:37.11]Briefly speaking,
[00:38.74]young people areinclined to idolize those 
[00:42.04]who excel in appearance or talent.
[00:45.36]For instance,
[00:47.28]Su Bingtian represents the image of a sports hero,
[00:51.56]whose handsome appearance and unparalleled skills are passionately 
[00:55.56]adored by his sports fans.
[00:59.80]By contrast,
[01:00.92]several teenage boys and girls win their fame overnight 
[01:04.92]in a great many contests on TV or online,
[01:07.57]which provokes noisy nationwide debates 
[01:10.57]on the value and harm of admiring these idols.
[01:15.00]In my opinion,
[01:16.22]idolatry is a double-edged sword which can exert a 
[01:19.22]profound influence for either good or bad on the growth of young people.
[01:25.61]If we endeavor to improve ourselves by bridging the gap
[01:28.61] between our role models and ourselves,
[01:31.65]we will lead a positive and fruitful life.
